results.summary:
Let T(n,k) = C(2n+1,2k+1)·C(2n-2k,n-k)/(n+1-k) = C(2n+1,2k+1)·Cat(n-k) for 0 ≤ k ≤ n, and T(n,k) = 0 for k > n. Let N(n,j) = C(n-1,j-1)·C(n,j-1)/j be the Narayana numbers, s = A001003 the little Schröder numbers, with s(0) = 1 and s(n) = Σ_{j=1..n} 2^{j-1} N(n,j) for n ≥ 1, and b = A103365 the first column of the inverse of the lower-unitriangular Narayana matrix, so that Σ_{j=1..n} N(n,j) b(j) = [n = 1]. Then:
(1) for every n ≥ 0, Σ_{k=0..⌊n/2⌋} T(n-k,k) = s(n+1);
(2) (corrected reading) for every n ≥ 0, Σ_{k=0..n} T(n,k)(2k+1)Cat(k)·b(k+1) = 0^n;
(3) for every N, the inverse over ℚ of the N×N leading block of T is the N×N leading block of U(n,k) = T(n,k)·b(n+1-k).

Let T(n,k) count domino towers (brick-wall pattern: a brick of layer i has a left end ≡ i mod 2 in half-brick units; the bottom layer is a contiguous row of b ≥ 1 bricks, normalised to start at 0; each brick above the base overlaps a brick below by at least half a brick) with n bricks and k layers. Then:
(1) for n > 1, T(n,2) = A000071(n+2) = F(n+2) - 1;
(2) for n ≥ 2, T(n,n-1) = A339252(n-2), where a(0)=1, a(1)=4, a(2)=11, a(m+3)=4a(m+2)-4a(m+1);
(3) for n ≥ 3, T(n,n-2) = A339254(n-3), where a(0..4)=1,7,24,70,193 and a(m+5)=6a(m+4)-12a(m+3)+8a(m+2);
(4) for n ≥ 4, T(n,n-3) = A339029(n-4), the coefficients of (1+4x-20x^2+8x^3+33x^4-4x^5-33x^6)/(1-2x)^4.
